# Split panes with tmux
> tmux splits one window into panes, each with its own shell, and keeps them running
> when you leave. It ships with UP-Term as UP-Term:tmux; if `which tmux` finds nothing, run
> the UP-Term installer again to update. tmux is slow to start on a 68k Amiga: wait for the prompt.
> This lesson works with a detached session called tutorial, so it never touches your own.
? Make a detached session called tutorial and split it into two panes.
= [ "$(tmux list-panes -t tutorial 2> /dev/null | grep -c .)" -eq 2 ]
~ Type tmux new-session -d -s tutorial && tmux split-window -t tutorial
! tmux new-session -d -s tutorial && tmux split-window -t tutorial
> The same split from the keyboard, inside tmux: Ctrl-B then % (side by side) or Ctrl-B
> then a double quote (stacked). Ctrl-B then d detaches and leaves everything running;
> `tmux attach` brings it back. Try them after this lesson.
? List the panes of the session with tmux list-panes -t tutorial.
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in "tmux list-panes -t tutorial") true ;; *) false ;; esac
= [ "$(grep -c . "$LEARN_OUT")" -eq 2 ]
~ Type tmux list-panes -t tutorial; there is one line per pane.
! tmux list-panes -t tutorial
? End the practice session with tmux kill-session -t tutorial.
= ! tmux has-session -t tutorial 2> /dev/null
~ Type tmux kill-session -t tutorial.
! tmux kill-session -t tutorial
